{"id": "774646ee-465f-46eb-822e-70e06dc59b9d", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T20:55:46.381260", "source": "user", "parent_id": "7f5373c0-5bea-46bb-9075-38a78bef5c25", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approved. Locking in your three scope-shaping answers with the following bindings, then proceed to the full design draft. (1) Prompting arms: exactly two cells — `ZeroShot` vs. `ZeroShot+CoT` (single reasoning trace, fixed-schema `Thought:` block, no self-consistency, no few-shot). Do not invent secondary arms. (2) Backbone: single frontier model as the sole actor. Since this is a design-only deliverable per the submission contract, leave the exact model ID as an explicit `<<MODEL_ID>>` placeholder to be filled by the researcher at execution time, and frame the design as 'single-actor evidence; cross-vendor replication deferred.' Do NOT pick a specific vendor or model name yourself. (3) Subset policy: rule (ii) — 10 tasks per L1/L2/L3 level = ~30 tasks total, one task per (level × ServiceNow-app) cell. Leave the exact 30-task enumeration as an explicit `<<TASK_ENUMERATION>>` placeholder; document the allocation rule but do not invent task names, IDs, or app counts. Everything else proceeds per your stated defaults: `n_trials = 3` paired within (task, trial-index); primary test = paired sign test with McNemar as backup; effect size = proportion-of-tasks-flipped plus absolute success-rate delta with 95% bootstrap CI; programmatic validators as primary ground truth with LLM-judge only as sensitivity (never judge-of-itself); `temperature = 0` (or vendor-equivalent) with provider version logged; DOM truncation cap held fixed across arms (state the cap as a parameter to be tuned, e.g. last 8k tokens of the accessibility tree, and log truncation rate per cell); CoT injected just-before-the-action-token in the user message with the fixed schema `Intent → Relevant observation → Predicted next state → Action`. The final design document must contain, at minimum: rationale tied to the question, full condition matrix with cell counts, randomization and paired-design mechanics, the exact statistical test with a power/coverage note for the chosen n_tasks and n_trials, the CoT prompt template (system vs. user injection point + fixed schema), controls and confound mitigations (truncation, temperature, contamination check via unseen/permutation sub-check, evaluator policy, judge-actor separation), the analysis plan (primary + secondary + disaggregated by L1/L2/L3), and an explicit feasibility/cost discussion making assumptions A2 (budget) and A3 (no GPU) prominent. Hard constraints carried into the draft: do NOT cite WorkArena++ task counts, L1/L2/L3 fractions, version pins, or baseline success rates from memory — if any such number appears, label it as `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`; do NOT invent or open the missing `neurips2024/97713/3:setup_1` reference; treat this strictly as a paper-style design deliverable and do not narrate fictitious runs. Deliver the full design now.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 21}
{"id": "a71927c8-f6d9-4d5a-81e2-4e40ca27541e", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T20:57:23.205911", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "774646ee-465f-46eb-822e-70e06dc59b9d", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Experimental Design — Does Chain-of-Thought Prompting Help LLM-based Agents on WorkArena++?\n\n**Deliverable type**: paper-style design document. No experiment is executed and no benchmark result is reported. All numeric, version, and enumeration facts that would normally depend on the WorkArena++ source are **explicit placeholders for the researcher** and labelled as such.\n\n**Document conventions**:\n- `<<PLACEHOLDER>>` = a value the researcher must supply at execution time.\n- `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>` = a number or fact that is not present in this workspace and that has **not been filled from memory**.\n- The missing research-topic file `neurips2024/97713/3:setup_1` is treated as **not in scope**; nothing below is sourced from it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research question and rationale\n\n**Question (literal).** Does chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting improve the task-solving ability of LLM-based agents on WorkArena++?\n\n**Question (operational).** Holding constant the agent loop, action grammar, observation format, truncation policy, sampling temperature, and per-decision compute, does appending a single chain-of-thought `Thought:` block before each action increase per-task success rate, on a stratified sample of WorkArena++ tasks, when the actor is a single frontier model `<<MODEL_ID>>`?\n\n**Why \"operational\" matters.** \"CoT helps\" is only attributable to CoT if every other known confounder is held fixed: action grammar, observation format, truncation, exemplars, sampling temperature, and per-decision budget. The design therefore (i) varies *only* the prompt structure between conditions, (ii) keeps n_trials identical, and (iii) randomizes at the trial level so any within-task outcome variance is shared across the two conditions by pairing.\n\n**Why paired, not independent-samples.** Tasks in WorkArena++ differ by orders of magnitude in difficulty. An unpaired comparison would conflate \"CoT helps\" with \"CoT happened to be tested on easier tasks\". Pairing on task identity removes between-task variance entirely and isolates the prompt effect.\n\n**Why one frontier model, single-actor.** Per assumption A3 and the researcher-approved binding, the bubblewrap has no GPU and no network, so an open-model arm cannot run. The result is **single-actor evidence**; cross-vendor generalization is explicitly deferred.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Conditions and cell counts\n\n### 2.1 Independent variable and factors\n- **Primary IV**: `prompt ∈ {ZeroShot, ZeroShot+CoT}`.\n- **Stratification factor**: `task difficulty ∈ {L1, L2, L3}` (per the benchmark's reported difficulty taxonomy — fractions not asserted here, see §2.3).\n- **Stratification factor**: `ServiceNow app` (one task per `(level × app)` cell, see §2.3).\n- **Random-effect factor**: `trial ∈ {1, 2, 3}` (paired within `(task, trial)`).\n\n### 2.2 Condition matrix (executed cells)\n\n|                       | L1                          | L2                          | L3                          | row total                   |\n|-----------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------|\n| **ZeroShot**          | 10 tasks × 3 trials = 30    | 10 tasks × 3 trials = 30    | 10 tasks × 3 trials = 30    | **30 tasks / 90 episodes**  |\n| **ZeroShot+CoT**      | 10 tasks × 3 trials = 30    | 10 tasks × 3 trials = 30    | 10 tasks × 3 trials = 30    | **30 tasks / 90 episodes**  |\n| **column total**      | 60 episodes                 | 60 episodes                 | 60 episodes                 | **180 episodes total**      |\n\nIf a chosen `(level, app)` cell has fewer than one programmatic-validator task in the researcher's task list, the missing slot is left empty and recorded in the audit table; the cell count of n_tasks=30 is a **target**, not a guarantee.\n\n### 2.3 Subset policy (locked)\n\n- **Allocation rule**: rule (ii) from the approval — **10 tasks per level**, **one task per `(level × ServiceNow app)` cell**. `<<TASK_ENUMERATION>>` is filled by the researcher; the design does not invent IDs, names, or app counts.\n- **Stratification target**: equal per-level counts (10/10/10), which means **level fractions are forced to 1/3 each** for this design. We do **not** assert that 1/3 ≈ published fractions; we explicitly state that this design departs from a published-fraction reweighting because those fractions are `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`.\n- Why cap at 10/level: it bounds episodes, makes per-level subgroup analysis feasible, and keeps the paired sign test (n=30 paired tasks) tractable.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Materials and setup\n\n| Element                | Choice                                                                                                                            | Source / placeholder                                                                       |\n|------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Benchmark              | WorkArena++ (subset per §2.3)                                                                                                       | `<<WORKARENA_PLUS_VERSION>>` `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`                            |\n| Agent framework        | BrowserGym, default action space and default system-prompt shell **except** the single modification specified in §4                  | `<<BROWSERGYM_VERSION>>` `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`                                |\n| Max-steps cap          | BrowserGym's published per-task default for WorkArena++                                                                              | `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>` `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`                                |\n| ServiceNow instance    | BrowserGym-managed, identical for every episode                                                                                     | identical across arms (held)                                                                |\n| Actor                  | Single frontier model `<<MODEL_ID>>`                                                                                                | researcher-supplied                                                                        |\n| Sampling               | `temperature = 0` (or vendor's \"deterministic\" equivalent), provider version logged once and pinned                               | logged                                                                                      |\n| Evaluator (primary)    | Programmatic WorkArena++ validators                                                                                                  | always primary; see §7.3                                                                   |\n| Evaluator (sensitivity)| LLM-judge validator — used **only** when a programmatic validator is absent, **never** with the actor model as judge                  | `<<JUDGE_MODEL_ID>>` `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>` (must ≠ actor)                    |\n| CoT injection point    | User message, **immediately before the action token**, after system prompt + observation                                          | fixed (see §4)                                                                              |\n| Truncation             | Last N tokens of the accessibility-tree observation, N identical across arms, truncation rate logged per cell                    | `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>` = 8000 (cap-as-parameter; tune jointly with `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>`) |\n\n---\n\n## 4. The CoT intervention (precise specification)\n\n### 4.1 Injection-point discipline\n- **System prompt**: identical in both arms. Holds the agent's role, action grammar, and general instructions. **No `thought` field exists in the system prompt.**\n- **User message**: identical in both arms *except* for the addition of a `Thought:` block immediately before the action token.\n- **Assistant turn**: produces exactly one response, which contains either (a) one action — in `ZeroShot`, or (b) one `Thought:` block followed by one action — in `ZeroShot+CoT`. No post-hoc edit by the agent; no scratchpad outside the schema below.\n\n### 4.2 Fixed `Thought:` schema (single trace per step)\nThe reasoning content is constrained to four labelled lines, in order:\n\n```\nThought:\n  Intent: <one short clause: what the agent is trying to do on this step>\n  Relevant observation: <quote or paraphrase the smallest piece of the current observation that justifies the action>\n  Predicted next state: <one short clause: the state the agent expects after the action>\n  Action: <the action token, syntactically valid under BrowserGym's grammar>\n```\n\nHard constraints:\n- A **`Thought:` block is required before every action** in the CoT arm (no skipping on \"obvious\" steps).\n- The block is **bounded** to ≤ `<<THOUGHT_TOKEN_BUDGET>>` tokens (default 256; tune down if total step budget underflows). This prevents CoT from double-counting as a self-consistency surrogate.\n- The block is **not echoed across steps**: the message history carries only the previous assistant turn(s); the *current* `Thought:` is produced fresh each step from the *current* observation.\n\n### 4.3 Why fixed-schema rather than free-form\nFree-form CoT is a moving target: any effect attributable to \"free-form CoT\" could equally be attributing it to length, hedging style, or token cost. Fixing the schema removes those confounds and gives us a clean lever: action grammar on/off × schema fixed/unfixed would be needed to address them, and that is out of scope.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Randomization and paired-design mechanics\n\n### 5.1 Trial alignment (the only way paired analysis is honest)\n- For each (task, model), `n_trials = 3` episodes are run in **each** prompting arm.\n- Trial index `t ∈ {1, 2, 3}` indexes an env-reset seed: `seed_t` is shared across the two arms. That is, `ZeroShot@seed_1` and `CoT@seed_1` start from identical browser state and observation.\n- Effectively: `90 × 2 = 180` episodes, of which `90` are paired on `seed_t`.\n\n### 5.2 Order randomization\n- Task order within each level is shuffled once and reused across arms (paired).\n- The order of `(ZeroShot, CoT)` execution is balanced per task to control for order effects: half the tasks run ZeroShot first, half run CoT first. Latin-square per level.\n\n### 5.3 Logging fields per episode\n- `task_id`, `level ∈ {L1, L2, L3}`, `app ∈ <<APP_ENUM>>`, `seed_t`, `prompt_arm`, `step_count`, `tokens_in`, `tokens_out`, `cost_usd`, `success_binary`, `validator_source ∈ {programmatic, llm_judge}`, `truncation_flag`, raw action trace, raw `Thought:` block (CoT arm only).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Statistical analysis plan\n\n### 6.1 Unit of analysis\n- **Primary unit**: the task. For each arm, the task-level outcome is the mean success across its 3 trials (`{0, 1/3, 2/3, 1}`). This gives **30 paired task-level observations**.\n- **Secondary unit**: the (task, trial) episode, giving 90 paired trials per arm-level cell. Used for diagnostics only.\n\n### 6.2 Primary test — paired sign test on the 30 task-level pairs\n- For each task `i ∈ {1..30}`, compute `s_i = success_CoT(i) − success_ZS(i) > 0` (treating ties as 0).\n- `B = Σ_i s_i` = number of tasks with a strictly positive mean-success delta.\n- Under H0 (`Pr(s_i = 1) = 0.5`), `B ~ Binomial(30, 0.5)` exactly.\n- **Rejection rule**: two-sided p = 2 · min(Pr(B ≥ b), Pr(B ≤ b)).\n- `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`: the exact-published L1/L2/L3 baseline success rates are not in scope; we do not pre-commit to an expected effect-size distribution.\n\n### 6.3 Backup test — exact McNemar on discordant tasks\n- Discordant pair = task where one arm is \"all-success\" and the other is \"not-all-success\" (or vice versa) at the trial-mean level.\n- Binomial test on the `2×2` discordant cell counts. McNemar is more powerful than the sign test when many tasks are \"stable\" on both arms (i.e., ceiling or floor), which is empirically plausible for web-agent benchmarks.\n- Discordant count threshold for two-sided p<0.05 with no continuity correction: `b` discordant cells such that `2 · sum_{k ≥ b} C(b+k, k) · 0.5^(b+k)` is small; equivalent in behaviour to a sign test restricted to ties-broken pairs.\n\n### 6.4 Effect sizes (always reported)\n- **Absolute success-rate delta** across the 30 tasks: `Δ = mean_i (success_CoT[i] − success_ZS[i])`, with 95% percentile-bootstrap CI over tasks (`B = 10000` resamples, paired).\n- **Proportion of tasks that flipped**: `b / 30`, reported per-direction (helped / hurt / no change), with binomial CI on `b`.\n- **Cohen's `h`** on the two overall success rates, reported for human-interpretability only — not used as the inferential target.\n\n### 6.5 Disaggregation (pre-planned)\n- Repeat the primary sign test **separately on L1, L2, L3 subsets** (each 10 paired tasks). This is the disaggregated analysis that lets us interpret where CoT does/does not help; with `n=10` per subset, **the per-subset sign test has very limited power** (rejection only if ≥ 9/10 tasks flip in the same direction), so the disaggregation is descriptive, not inferential. Bonferroni-corrected α not applied across levels (levels are pre-specified subgroup hypotheses, not post-hoc).\n- Repeat the primary sign test per `(level × app)` cell with `n ≤ 10` — flagged as **exploratory**, no inferential claims.\n\n### 6.6 Power / coverage note\nWith 30 paired task-level observations:\n- **Sign test minimum detectable effect at the task level**: for two-sided α=0.05, the rejection region is `B ≥ 21` (i.e., ≥ 21/30 tasks must flip in the same direction). Approximate power:\n  - If the true proportion of positively-flipping tasks is `p = 0.70`, `Pr(Bin(30, 0.70) ≥ 21) ≈ 0.55`.\n  - At `p = 0.80`, power `≈ 0.82`.\n  - At `p = 0.75`, power `≈ 0.71`.\n  - Honest reading: **moderately-sized effects (~70–80 % of tasks flipping the same direction)** are required for a sign-test rejection at n=30; smaller, real effects will not reach significance and the result will read as \"no effect\" rather than \"small effect\". This is a real limitation, not a corner-case, and is the main argument for keeping the McNemar backup.\n- **Bootstrap CI on Δ**: with n=30 resampling at the task level, the CI half-width on a difference of two Bernoulli means is roughly `2·√(p_avg·(1−p_avg)/30)`. For `p_avg ≈ 0.4`, half-width `≈ 0.18` — wide enough that small or moderate effects will be CI-compatible with zero. Reported but not used as a strict decision rule.\n- **Multiplicative effect of `n_trials = 3`**: trials reduce the *variance* of the per-task success estimate but not the per-task count. They give the CoT arm enough \"room to fail without losing information\" — i.e., if a task is solvable, `n_trials=3` reduces the chance of a false-zero caused by a single unlucky episode.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Controls and confound mitigations\n\n### 7.1 Truncation (DOM-observation cap)\n- **Policy**: keep the *last* `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>` = 8000 tokens of the accessibility-tree observation in every step, in both arms.\n- **Logged**: `truncation_flag` (boolean) and `truncated_tokens` count per episode.\n- **Reported**: truncation rate per `(arm × level)` cell. If they diverge, the success-rate delta is suspect and is reported with and without the truncation-flagged episodes.\n\n### 7.2 Temperature / sampling\n- `temperature = 0` (or vendor \"deterministic\"), seeded where supported.\n- Provider version, model revision, and any auxiliary sampling flags are recorded **once** at the start of the run and pinned for its duration.\n- `n_trials = 3` is not a substitute for non-determinism control; it is a stability check. We do not assume identical outputs across providers at \"deterministic\" settings.\n\n### 7.3 Evaluator policy and judge-actor separation\n- **Primary ground truth**: WorkArena++ programmatic validator. Per-episode success is the validator output.\n- **Sensitivity**: if a chosen task has no programmatic validator, an LLM-judge validator is used **only when the judge model is explicitly different from the actor model `<<MODEL_ID>>`**. Judge-actor separation is mandatory.\n- The LLM-judge output is reported **alongside** the programmatic output where both exist; their agreement (Cohen's κ) is reported. If κ < `<<KAPPA_THRESHOLD>>` (placeholder, default 0.6), the corresponding task is downgraded to \"exploratory only\".\n\n### 7.4 Contamination sub-check\nWe cannot rule out that `<<MODEL_ID>>` has been exposed to WorkArena / WorkArena++ tasks during pre-training. A pure contamination \"test\" would require an unseen benchmark that does not exist. Instead:\n- **Permutation sub-check** on a random sample of `<<N_PERMUTATION_TASKS>>` (placeholder, default 5) tasks drawn from the chosen subset: each task's UI is given one independent pre-registered mutation (e.g., a form-field label reworded, an answer choice order shuffled). If the CoT arm's success on the *original* task is at ceiling while the **mutation** drops success for both arms, this is consistent with genuine task-solving rather than memorization. If the *original* task shows ceiling success and the **mutation** also shows ceiling success *only* in the CoT arm, the result is flagged as possible contamination-driven ceiling.\n- A higher-than-99 % per-task success rate triggers a **mandatory** permutation on that task before its result is published.\n\n### 7.5 Logging schema (minimum)\nPer episode: identifiers (§5.3). Per run: model revision, provider version, sampling config, truncation cap, validator source, judge model. Per analysis: freeze the analysis script version and the cell count.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Hypotheses to pre-register (qualitative, before any seen result)\n\nThe following are pre-specified *directions*, not effect-size predictions. We do not pre-commit to (H1) being true; the design's job is to discriminate, not to confirm.\n\n- **H1 (primary, overall).** The CoT arm's task-level mean success is non-trivially higher than ZeroShot's, with the effect-size dominated by the L2/L3 subgroups.\n- **H2 (direction by level).** The proportion of tasks that flip in favour of CoT is monotonically non-decreasing with difficulty: `p_flip(L1) ≤ p_flip(L2) ≤ p_flip(L3)`. (Reversing direction would falsify the rationale.)\n- **H3 (cost).** Mean cost per solved task may *increase* in the CoT arm even if success improves — CoT adds tokens per step and may add steps if it occasionally second-guesses correct actions. We will report cost per solved task, not just success rate.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Feasibility, cost, and resource discussion (assumptions A2 and A3 explicit)\n\n### 9.1 A3 — no GPU, no network (forced single-actor scope)\n- The bubblewrap has no GPU and network is disabled. An open-model arm requires either a local GPU (not available) or a hosted OpenAI-compatible endpoint (network required).\n- **Consequence**: this design produces **single-actor evidence only**. The headline conclusion is of the form *\"For actor `<<MODEL_ID>>`, CoT prompting does/does not increase success rate on a subset of WorkArena++.\"* Cross-vendor, cross-size, and open-model generalization are explicitly out of scope.\n- **What we cannot conclude from this run**: that CoT helps or hurts in absolute terms across model families; that an open model would respond similarly; that \"more reasoning tokens\" would monotonically improve.\n\n### 9.2 A2 — API budget unstated\n- **Token accounting (framework, not numbers)**: per-episode token spend is `(prompt_tokens_per_step × steps_per_episode) + (output_tokens_per_step × steps_per_episode)`. In the CoT arm, the output side carries the `Thought:` block, which costs approximately `<<THOUGHT_TOKEN_BUDGET>>` (default 256) tokens per step on top of the action.\n- **Without exact per-step-token and per-step-cost numbers** (which depend on `<<MODEL_ID>>`, `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>`, and `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>`, all placeholders), we give a budget formula:\n\n```\ntotal_spend ≈ 180 episodes × mean_steps × (in_token_price·in_tokens/step + out_token_price·(out_tokens/step + <<THOUGHT_TOKEN_BUDGET>>)_CoT_only)\n```\n\n- **Budget-control levers**, in order of preference: reduce `n_trials` from 3 to 1 (halves episodes to 60 per arm); shrink `n_tasks_per_level` from 10 to 5 (halves again); truncate `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>` further; cap `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>` below the default. Each lever weakens an inferential lever; the order above preserves the design's cleanest causal claim while shrinking cost.\n- **`<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`**: per-token price, expected mean step count, and the ceiling spend are all researcher-supplied; we do not invent them.\n\n### 9.3 Wall-clock feasibility\n- 180 episodes × mean episode wall-clock (depends on `<<MODEL_ID>>` latency and page-load time) — order of magnitude is researcher-driven. A practical feasibility rule: if a single 90-episode arm exceeds the budgeted time by more than the researcher can tolerate, drop `n_trials` to 2 first, then reduce `n_tasks_per_level`.\n\n### 9.4 Failure-mode transparency (what we will say if things go wrong)\n- If a task fails to load on the BrowserGym instance, the task's cell is recorded as \"infra-failure\" and excluded from both arms' analyses. The audit table reports the exclusion.\n- If a programmatic validator is flagged as broken (multiple consecutive episodes fail in the same way on a \"trivial\" action), that task is downgraded to exploratory.\n- If per-trial variance for any task is ≥ 2/3 (i.e., outcomes are `{0, 0, 1}` or similar), the task is flagged as \"high-variance\" and reported with and without inclusion.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Out of scope (explicit)\n\n- Secondary prompting arms: few-shot CoT, self-consistency, \"let's think step by step\" with no fixed schema.\n- Open-model actor (deferred; see §1, §9.1).\n- Cross-vendor generalization (deferred).\n- Full WorkArena++ benchmark coverage (deferred; subset per §2.3).\n- Inference-time prompt optimization / dynamic exemplar selection.\n- Comparison of `Thought:` schemas (only one schema is tested).\n- Cost-optimized model selection (model is fixed by researcher).\n\n---\n\n## 11. Researcher-supplied inputs at execution time\n\nThe design is complete and self-consistent **conditional on the following**:\n\n| Placeholder                                | Meaning                                                                                                  |\n|--------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `<<MODEL_ID>>`                             | Frontier-language-model identifier used as the sole actor. Must satisfy A3 (no GPU, no network).         |\n| `<<JUDGE_MODEL_ID>>`                       | LLM-judge model, when used for sensitivity. Must be ≠ `<<MODEL_ID>>`.                                    |\n| `<<TASK_ENUMERATION>>`                     | The 30-task list, 10 per L1/L2/L3, distribution rule per §2.3.                                            |\n| `<<APP_ENUM>>`                             | ServiceNow-app enumeration adopted from the WorkArena++ task taxonomy (filled by researcher).            |\n| `<<BROWSERGYM_VERSION>>`                   | BrowserGym version pin.                                                                                   |\n| `<<WORKARENA_PLUS_VERSION>>`               | WorkArena++ release pin.                                                                                  |\n| `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>`                    | Per-task step cap.                                                                                        |\n| `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>`                | Observation truncation cap (default 8000).                                                                |\n| `<<THOUGHT_TOKEN_BUDGET>>`                  | Maximum tokens spent on the `Thought:` block per step (default 256).                                      |\n| `<<KAPPA_THRESHOLD>>`                      | Judge-vs-programmatic κ below which a task is downgraded (default 0.6).                                   |\n| `<<N_PERMUTATION_TASKS>>`                  | Number of tasks on which the contamination permutation sub-check is run (default 5).                      |\n| `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>` block   | Any benchmark number (task count, L1/L2/L3 fraction, baseline success rate, etc.) — never asserted.       |\n\n**Everything above is design, not evidence.** No run was executed; no episode traces, success rates, step counts, or token costs are reported.\n", "evidence": "All design choices are grounded in (i) the workspace inspection from turn 1 (`find /workspace -type f` returned only `task.json`; no WorkArena++, BrowserGym, or related package present; no GPU; network disabled in bubblewrap; `pip3 list` shows no LLM/agent SDKs); (ii) the researcher-approved bindings on arms (exactly two), models (single frontier, identifier left as `<<MODEL_ID>>`), and subset (rule (ii), 10 per level, enumeration left as `<<TASK_ENUMERATION>>`); (iii) the design's own paired-MacNemar-plus-sign-test defence for binary outcomes and the n=30 power computation stated explicitly. No benchmark counts, fractions, version pins, or baseline rates are cited from memory; each appears as a labelled placeholder `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`. The missing `neurips2024/97713/3:setup_1` is treated as not in scope and is not invented.", "assumptions": ["A1. Expected empirical outcome is unknown; H1/H2/H3 are qualitative directions, not parameter-value predictions.", "A2. API budget, per-token prices, and per-episode wall-clock are unstated; §9.2 gives a parametric budget but no numeric total.", "A3. No GPU + no network in bubblewrap ⇒ open-model arm is structurally excluded; design is single-actor, cross-vendor deferred.", "A4. BrowserGym's default prompt does not already contain a `Thought:` field; the user-message injection point is therefore additive. If the researcher's reflection on `<<BROWSERGYM_VERSION>>` shows otherwise, §4.1 must be revised to a syntactic-prefix intervention.", "A5. Programmatic validators exist for the chosen 30 tasks; if any task lacks one, it falls to sensitivity per §7.3.", "A6. n_trials=3 paired within (task, seed) provides honest per-task success aggregation; we do not assume n_trials=3 eliminates all API non-determinism.", "A7. `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>`=8000 is a parameter, not a fact: divergence in truncation rate across arms is a primary confound-mitigation concern (§7.1).", "A8. L1/L2/L3 fractions in the published benchmark are not asserted; the design's 1/3-per-level split is a methodological choice, not a claim about the published mix."], "uncertainties": ["U1. The exact `<<MODEL_ID>>` and `<<JUDGE_MODEL_ID>>` (they must differ).", "U2. The exact `<<TASK_ENUMERATION>>` over `(level × app)` cells.", "U3. `<<BROWSERGYM_VERSION>>`, `<<WORKARENA_PLUS_VERSION>>`, `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>` — every numeric reference to the framework is placeholder.", "U4. Token price, latency, and budget ceiling — no numeric total can be promised.", "U5. Whether the chosen 30-task subset is fully covered by programmatic validators; if not, §7.3 governs.", "U6. Whether the researcher's submission surface expects an analysis script skeleton alongside the design — none is requested here but can be added in a follow-up.", "U7. Whether `track.md` imposes hard caps on design-document length or section count beyond what has been provided."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-ba5bd134-5c60-4013-8eee-5accb1f931a1", "tool_call": {"id": "call-ba5bd134-5c60-4013-8eee-5accb1f931a1", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Deliver full design document for CoT experiment\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Experimental Design \\u2014 Does Chain-of-Thought Prompting Help LLM-based Agents on WorkArena++?\\n\\n**Deliverable type**: paper-style design document. No experiment is executed and no benchmark result is reported. All numeric, version, and enumeration facts that would normally depend on the WorkArena++ source are **explicit placeholders for the researcher** and labelled as such.\\n\\n**Document conventions**:\\n- `<<PLACEHOLDER>>` = a value the researcher must supply at execution time.\\n- `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>` = a number or fact that is not present in this workspace and that has **not been filled from memory**.\\n- The missing research-topic file `neurips2024/97713/3:setup_1` is treated as **not in scope**; nothing below is sourced from it.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Research question and rationale\\n\\n**Question (literal).** Does chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting improve the task-solving ability of LLM-based agents on WorkArena++?\\n\\n**Question (operational).** Holding constant the agent loop, action grammar, observation format, truncation policy, sampling temperature, and per-decision compute, does appending a single chain-of-thought `Thought:` block before each action increase per-task success rate, on a stratified sample of WorkArena++ tasks, when the actor is a single frontier model `<<MODEL_ID>>`?\\n\\n**Why \\\"operational\\\" matters.** \\\"CoT helps\\\" is only attributable to CoT if every other known confounder is held fixed: action grammar, observation format, truncation, exemplars, sampling temperature, and per-decision budget. The design therefore (i) varies *only* the prompt structure between conditions, (ii) keeps n_trials identical, and (iii) randomizes at the trial level so any within-task outcome variance is shared across the two conditions by pairing.\\n\\n**Why paired, not independent-samples.** Tasks in WorkArena++ differ by orders of magnitude in difficulty. An unpaired comparison would conflate \\\"CoT helps\\\" with \\\"CoT happened to be tested on easier tasks\\\". Pairing on task identity removes between-task variance entirely and isolates the prompt effect.\\n\\n**Why one frontier model, single-actor.** Per assumption A3 and the researcher-approved binding, the bubblewrap has no GPU and no network, so an open-model arm cannot run. The result is **single-actor evidence**; cross-vendor generalization is explicitly deferred.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Conditions and cell counts\\n\\n### 2.1 Independent variable and factors\\n- **Primary IV**: `prompt \\u2208 {ZeroShot, ZeroShot+CoT}`.\\n- **Stratification factor**: `task difficulty \\u2208 {L1, L2, L3}` (per the benchmark's reported difficulty taxonomy \\u2014 fractions not asserted here, see \\u00a72.3).\\n- **Stratification factor**: `ServiceNow app` (one task per `(level \\u00d7 app)` cell, see \\u00a72.3).\\n- **Random-effect factor**: `trial \\u2208 {1, 2, 3}` (paired within `(task, trial)`).\\n\\n### 2.2 Condition matrix (executed cells)\\n\\n|                       | L1                          | L2                          | L3                          | row total                   |\\n|-----------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------|\\n| **ZeroShot**          | 10 tasks \\u00d7 3 trials = 30    | 10 tasks \\u00d7 3 trials = 30    | 10 tasks \\u00d7 3 trials = 30    | **30 tasks / 90 episodes**  |\\n| **ZeroShot+CoT**      | 10 tasks \\u00d7 3 trials = 30    | 10 tasks \\u00d7 3 trials = 30    | 10 tasks \\u00d7 3 trials = 30    | **30 tasks / 90 episodes**  |\\n| **column total**      | 60 episodes                 | 60 episodes                 | 60 episodes                 | **180 episodes total**      |\\n\\nIf a chosen `(level, app)` cell has fewer than one programmatic-validator task in the researcher's task list, the missing slot is left empty and recorded in the audit table; the cell count of n_tasks=30 is a **target**, not a guarantee.\\n\\n### 2.3 Subset policy (locked)\\n\\n- **Allocation rule**: rule (ii) from the approval \\u2014 **10 tasks per level**, **one task per `(level \\u00d7 ServiceNow app)` cell**. `<<TASK_ENUMERATION>>` is filled by the researcher; the design does not invent IDs, names, or app counts.\\n- **Stratification target**: equal per-level counts (10/10/10), which means **level fractions are forced to 1/3 each** for this design. We do **not** assert that 1/3 \\u2248 published fractions; we explicitly state that this design departs from a published-fraction reweighting because those fractions are `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`.\\n- Why cap at 10/level: it bounds episodes, makes per-level subgroup analysis feasible, and keeps the paired sign test (n=30 paired tasks) tractable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Materials and setup\\n\\n| Element                | Choice                                                                                                                            | Source / placeholder                                                                       |\\n|------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\n| Benchmark              | WorkArena++ (subset per \\u00a72.3)                                                                                                       | `<<WORKARENA_PLUS_VERSION>>` `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`                            |\\n| Agent framework        | BrowserGym, default action space and default system-prompt shell **except** the single modification specified in \\u00a74                  | `<<BROWSERGYM_VERSION>>` `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`                                |\\n| Max-steps cap          | BrowserGym's published per-task default for WorkArena++                                                                              | `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>` `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`                                |\\n| ServiceNow instance    | BrowserGym-managed, identical for every episode                                                                                     | identical across arms (held)                                                                |\\n| Actor                  | Single frontier model `<<MODEL_ID>>`                                                                                                | researcher-supplied                                                                        |\\n| Sampling               | `temperature = 0` (or vendor's \\\"deterministic\\\" equivalent), provider version logged once and pinned                               | logged                                                                                      |\\n| Evaluator (primary)    | Programmatic WorkArena++ validators                                                                                                  | always primary; see \\u00a77.3                                                                   |\\n| Evaluator (sensitivity)| LLM-judge validator \\u2014 used **only** when a programmatic validator is absent, **never** with the actor model as judge                  | `<<JUDGE_MODEL_ID>>` `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>` (must \\u2260 actor)                    |\\n| CoT injection point    | User message, **immediately before the action token**, after system prompt + observation                                          | fixed (see \\u00a74)                                                                              |\\n| Truncation             | Last N tokens of the accessibility-tree observation, N identical across arms, truncation rate logged per cell                    | `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>` = 8000 (cap-as-parameter; tune jointly with `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>`) |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. The CoT intervention (precise specification)\\n\\n### 4.1 Injection-point discipline\\n- **System prompt**: identical in both arms. Holds the agent's role, action grammar, and general instructions. **No `thought` field exists in the system prompt.**\\n- **User message**: identical in both arms *except* for the addition of a `Thought:` block immediately before the action token.\\n- **Assistant turn**: produces exactly one response, which contains either (a) one action \\u2014 in `ZeroShot`, or (b) one `Thought:` block followed by one action \\u2014 in `ZeroShot+CoT`. No post-hoc edit by the agent; no scratchpad outside the schema below.\\n\\n### 4.2 Fixed `Thought:` schema (single trace per step)\\nThe reasoning content is constrained to four labelled lines, in order:\\n\\n```\\nThought:\\n  Intent: <one short clause: what the agent is trying to do on this step>\\n  Relevant observation: <quote or paraphrase the smallest piece of the current observation that justifies the action>\\n  Predicted next state: <one short clause: the state the agent expects after the action>\\n  Action: <the action token, syntactically valid under BrowserGym's grammar>\\n```\\n\\nHard constraints:\\n- A **`Thought:` block is required before every action** in the CoT arm (no skipping on \\\"obvious\\\" steps).\\n- The block is **bounded** to \\u2264 `<<THOUGHT_TOKEN_BUDGET>>` tokens (default 256; tune down if total step budget underflows). This prevents CoT from double-counting as a self-consistency surrogate.\\n- The block is **not echoed across steps**: the message history carries only the previous assistant turn(s); the *current* `Thought:` is produced fresh each step from the *current* observation.\\n\\n### 4.3 Why fixed-schema rather than free-form\\nFree-form CoT is a moving target: any effect attributable to \\\"free-form CoT\\\" could equally be attributing it to length, hedging style, or token cost. Fixing the schema removes those confounds and gives us a clean lever: action grammar on/off \\u00d7 schema fixed/unfixed would be needed to address them, and that is out of scope.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Randomization and paired-design mechanics\\n\\n### 5.1 Trial alignment (the only way paired analysis is honest)\\n- For each (task, model), `n_trials = 3` episodes are run in **each** prompting arm.\\n- Trial index `t \\u2208 {1, 2, 3}` indexes an env-reset seed: `seed_t` is shared across the two arms. That is, `ZeroShot@seed_1` and `CoT@seed_1` start from identical browser state and observation.\\n- Effectively: `90 \\u00d7 2 = 180` episodes, of which `90` are paired on `seed_t`.\\n\\n### 5.2 Order randomization\\n- Task order within each level is shuffled once and reused across arms (paired).\\n- The order of `(ZeroShot, CoT)` execution is balanced per task to control for order effects: half the tasks run ZeroShot first, half run CoT first. Latin-square per level.\\n\\n### 5.3 Logging fields per episode\\n- `task_id`, `level \\u2208 {L1, L2, L3}`, `app \\u2208 <<APP_ENUM>>`, `seed_t`, `prompt_arm`, `step_count`, `tokens_in`, `tokens_out`, `cost_usd`, `success_binary`, `validator_source \\u2208 {programmatic, llm_judge}`, `truncation_flag`, raw action trace, raw `Thought:` block (CoT arm only).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Statistical analysis plan\\n\\n### 6.1 Unit of analysis\\n- **Primary unit**: the task. For each arm, the task-level outcome is the mean success across its 3 trials (`{0, 1/3, 2/3, 1}`). This gives **30 paired task-level observations**.\\n- **Secondary unit**: the (task, trial) episode, giving 90 paired trials per arm-level cell. Used for diagnostics only.\\n\\n### 6.2 Primary test \\u2014 paired sign test on the 30 task-level pairs\\n- For each task `i \\u2208 {1..30}`, compute `s_i = success_CoT(i) \\u2212 success_ZS(i) > 0` (treating ties as 0).\\n- `B = \\u03a3_i s_i` = number of tasks with a strictly positive mean-success delta.\\n- Under H0 (`Pr(s_i = 1) = 0.5`), `B ~ Binomial(30, 0.5)` exactly.\\n- **Rejection rule**: two-sided p = 2 \\u00b7 min(Pr(B \\u2265 b), Pr(B \\u2264 b)).\\n- `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`: the exact-published L1/L2/L3 baseline success rates are not in scope; we do not pre-commit to an expected effect-size distribution.\\n\\n### 6.3 Backup test \\u2014 exact McNemar on discordant tasks\\n- Discordant pair = task where one arm is \\\"all-success\\\" and the other is \\\"not-all-success\\\" (or vice versa) at the trial-mean level.\\n- Binomial test on the `2\\u00d72` discordant cell counts. McNemar is more powerful than the sign test when many tasks are \\\"stable\\\" on both arms (i.e., ceiling or floor), which is empirically plausible for web-agent benchmarks.\\n- Discordant count threshold for two-sided p<0.05 with no continuity correction: `b` discordant cells such that `2 \\u00b7 sum_{k \\u2265 b} C(b+k, k) \\u00b7 0.5^(b+k)` is small; equivalent in behaviour to a sign test restricted to ties-broken pairs.\\n\\n### 6.4 Effect sizes (always reported)\\n- **Absolute success-rate delta** across the 30 tasks: `\\u0394 = mean_i (success_CoT[i] \\u2212 success_ZS[i])`, with 95% percentile-bootstrap CI over tasks (`B = 10000` resamples, paired).\\n- **Proportion of tasks that flipped**: `b / 30`, reported per-direction (helped / hurt / no change), with binomial CI on `b`.\\n- **Cohen's `h`** on the two overall success rates, reported for human-interpretability only \\u2014 not used as the inferential target.\\n\\n### 6.5 Disaggregation (pre-planned)\\n- Repeat the primary sign test **separately on L1, L2, L3 subsets** (each 10 paired tasks). This is the disaggregated analysis that lets us interpret where CoT does/does not help; with `n=10` per subset, **the per-subset sign test has very limited power** (rejection only if \\u2265 9/10 tasks flip in the same direction), so the disaggregation is descriptive, not inferential. Bonferroni-corrected \\u03b1 not applied across levels (levels are pre-specified subgroup hypotheses, not post-hoc).\\n- Repeat the primary sign test per `(level \\u00d7 app)` cell with `n \\u2264 10` \\u2014 flagged as **exploratory**, no inferential claims.\\n\\n### 6.6 Power / coverage note\\nWith 30 paired task-level observations:\\n- **Sign test minimum detectable effect at the task level**: for two-sided \\u03b1=0.05, the rejection region is `B \\u2265 21` (i.e., \\u2265 21/30 tasks must flip in the same direction). Approximate power:\\n  - If the true proportion of positively-flipping tasks is `p = 0.70`, `Pr(Bin(30, 0.70) \\u2265 21) \\u2248 0.55`.\\n  - At `p = 0.80`, power `\\u2248 0.82`.\\n  - At `p = 0.75`, power `\\u2248 0.71`.\\n  - Honest reading: **moderately-sized effects (~70\\u201380 % of tasks flipping the same direction)** are required for a sign-test rejection at n=30; smaller, real effects will not reach significance and the result will read as \\\"no effect\\\" rather than \\\"small effect\\\". This is a real limitation, not a corner-case, and is the main argument for keeping the McNemar backup.\\n- **Bootstrap CI on \\u0394**: with n=30 resampling at the task level, the CI half-width on a difference of two Bernoulli means is roughly `2\\u00b7\\u221a(p_avg\\u00b7(1\\u2212p_avg)/30)`. For `p_avg \\u2248 0.4`, half-width `\\u2248 0.18` \\u2014 wide enough that small or moderate effects will be CI-compatible with zero. Reported but not used as a strict decision rule.\\n- **Multiplicative effect of `n_trials = 3`**: trials reduce the *variance* of the per-task success estimate but not the per-task count. They give the CoT arm enough \\\"room to fail without losing information\\\" \\u2014 i.e., if a task is solvable, `n_trials=3` reduces the chance of a false-zero caused by a single unlucky episode.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Controls and confound mitigations\\n\\n### 7.1 Truncation (DOM-observation cap)\\n- **Policy**: keep the *last* `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>` = 8000 tokens of the accessibility-tree observation in every step, in both arms.\\n- **Logged**: `truncation_flag` (boolean) and `truncated_tokens` count per episode.\\n- **Reported**: truncation rate per `(arm \\u00d7 level)` cell. If they diverge, the success-rate delta is suspect and is reported with and without the truncation-flagged episodes.\\n\\n### 7.2 Temperature / sampling\\n- `temperature = 0` (or vendor \\\"deterministic\\\"), seeded where supported.\\n- Provider version, model revision, and any auxiliary sampling flags are recorded **once** at the start of the run and pinned for its duration.\\n- `n_trials = 3` is not a substitute for non-determinism control; it is a stability check. We do not assume identical outputs across providers at \\\"deterministic\\\" settings.\\n\\n### 7.3 Evaluator policy and judge-actor separation\\n- **Primary ground truth**: WorkArena++ programmatic validator. Per-episode success is the validator output.\\n- **Sensitivity**: if a chosen task has no programmatic validator, an LLM-judge validator is used **only when the judge model is explicitly different from the actor model `<<MODEL_ID>>`**. Judge-actor separation is mandatory.\\n- The LLM-judge output is reported **alongside** the programmatic output where both exist; their agreement (Cohen's \\u03ba) is reported. If \\u03ba < `<<KAPPA_THRESHOLD>>` (placeholder, default 0.6), the corresponding task is downgraded to \\\"exploratory only\\\".\\n\\n### 7.4 Contamination sub-check\\nWe cannot rule out that `<<MODEL_ID>>` has been exposed to WorkArena / WorkArena++ tasks during pre-training. A pure contamination \\\"test\\\" would require an unseen benchmark that does not exist. Instead:\\n- **Permutation sub-check** on a random sample of `<<N_PERMUTATION_TASKS>>` (placeholder, default 5) tasks drawn from the chosen subset: each task's UI is given one independent pre-registered mutation (e.g., a form-field label reworded, an answer choice order shuffled). If the CoT arm's success on the *original* task is at ceiling while the **mutation** drops success for both arms, this is consistent with genuine task-solving rather than memorization. If the *original* task shows ceiling success and the **mutation** also shows ceiling success *only* in the CoT arm, the result is flagged as possible contamination-driven ceiling.\\n- A higher-than-99 % per-task success rate triggers a **mandatory** permutation on that task before its result is published.\\n\\n### 7.5 Logging schema (minimum)\\nPer episode: identifiers (\\u00a75.3). Per run: model revision, provider version, sampling config, truncation cap, validator source, judge model. Per analysis: freeze the analysis script version and the cell count.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Hypotheses to pre-register (qualitative, before any seen result)\\n\\nThe following are pre-specified *directions*, not effect-size predictions. We do not pre-commit to (H1) being true; the design's job is to discriminate, not to confirm.\\n\\n- **H1 (primary, overall).** The CoT arm's task-level mean success is non-trivially higher than ZeroShot's, with the effect-size dominated by the L2/L3 subgroups.\\n- **H2 (direction by level).** The proportion of tasks that flip in favour of CoT is monotonically non-decreasing with difficulty: `p_flip(L1) \\u2264 p_flip(L2) \\u2264 p_flip(L3)`. (Reversing direction would falsify the rationale.)\\n- **H3 (cost).** Mean cost per solved task may *increase* in the CoT arm even if success improves \\u2014 CoT adds tokens per step and may add steps if it occasionally second-guesses correct actions. We will report cost per solved task, not just success rate.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Feasibility, cost, and resource discussion (assumptions A2 and A3 explicit)\\n\\n### 9.1 A3 \\u2014 no GPU, no network (forced single-actor scope)\\n- The bubblewrap has no GPU and network is disabled. An open-model arm requires either a local GPU (not available) or a hosted OpenAI-compatible endpoint (network required).\\n- **Consequence**: this design produces **single-actor evidence only**. The headline conclusion is of the form *\\\"For actor `<<MODEL_ID>>`, CoT prompting does/does not increase success rate on a subset of WorkArena++.\\\"* Cross-vendor, cross-size, and open-model generalization are explicitly out of scope.\\n- **What we cannot conclude from this run**: that CoT helps or hurts in absolute terms across model families; that an open model would respond similarly; that \\\"more reasoning tokens\\\" would monotonically improve.\\n\\n### 9.2 A2 \\u2014 API budget unstated\\n- **Token accounting (framework, not numbers)**: per-episode token spend is `(prompt_tokens_per_step \\u00d7 steps_per_episode) + (output_tokens_per_step \\u00d7 steps_per_episode)`. In the CoT arm, the output side carries the `Thought:` block, which costs approximately `<<THOUGHT_TOKEN_BUDGET>>` (default 256) tokens per step on top of the action.\\n- **Without exact per-step-token and per-step-cost numbers** (which depend on `<<MODEL_ID>>`, `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>`, and `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>`, all placeholders), we give a budget formula:\\n\\n```\\ntotal_spend \\u2248 180 episodes \\u00d7 mean_steps \\u00d7 (in_token_price\\u00b7in_tokens/step + out_token_price\\u00b7(out_tokens/step + <<THOUGHT_TOKEN_BUDGET>>)_CoT_only)\\n```\\n\\n- **Budget-control levers**, in order of preference: reduce `n_trials` from 3 to 1 (halves episodes to 60 per arm); shrink `n_tasks_per_level` from 10 to 5 (halves again); truncate `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>` further; cap `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>` below the default. Each lever weakens an inferential lever; the order above preserves the design's cleanest causal claim while shrinking cost.\\n- **`<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`**: per-token price, expected mean step count, and the ceiling spend are all researcher-supplied; we do not invent them.\\n\\n### 9.3 Wall-clock feasibility\\n- 180 episodes \\u00d7 mean episode wall-clock (depends on `<<MODEL_ID>>` latency and page-load time) \\u2014 order of magnitude is researcher-driven. A practical feasibility rule: if a single 90-episode arm exceeds the budgeted time by more than the researcher can tolerate, drop `n_trials` to 2 first, then reduce `n_tasks_per_level`.\\n\\n### 9.4 Failure-mode transparency (what we will say if things go wrong)\\n- If a task fails to load on the BrowserGym instance, the task's cell is recorded as \\\"infra-failure\\\" and excluded from both arms' analyses. The audit table reports the exclusion.\\n- If a programmatic validator is flagged as broken (multiple consecutive episodes fail in the same way on a \\\"trivial\\\" action), that task is downgraded to exploratory.\\n- If per-trial variance for any task is \\u2265 2/3 (i.e., outcomes are `{0, 0, 1}` or similar), the task is flagged as \\\"high-variance\\\" and reported with and without inclusion.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Out of scope (explicit)\\n\\n- Secondary prompting arms: few-shot CoT, self-consistency, \\\"let's think step by step\\\" with no fixed schema.\\n- Open-model actor (deferred; see \\u00a71, \\u00a79.1).\\n- Cross-vendor generalization (deferred).\\n- Full WorkArena++ benchmark coverage (deferred; subset per \\u00a72.3).\\n- Inference-time prompt optimization / dynamic exemplar selection.\\n- Comparison of `Thought:` schemas (only one schema is tested).\\n- Cost-optimized model selection (model is fixed by researcher).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Researcher-supplied inputs at execution time\\n\\nThe design is complete and self-consistent **conditional on the following**:\\n\\n| Placeholder                                | Meaning                                                                                                  |\\n|--------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\\n| `<<MODEL_ID>>`                             | Frontier-language-model identifier used as the sole actor. Must satisfy A3 (no GPU, no network).         |\\n| `<<JUDGE_MODEL_ID>>`                       | LLM-judge model, when used for sensitivity. Must be \\u2260 `<<MODEL_ID>>`.                                    |\\n| `<<TASK_ENUMERATION>>`                     | The 30-task list, 10 per L1/L2/L3, distribution rule per \\u00a72.3.                                            |\\n| `<<APP_ENUM>>`                             | ServiceNow-app enumeration adopted from the WorkArena++ task taxonomy (filled by researcher).            |\\n| `<<BROWSERGYM_VERSION>>`                   | BrowserGym version pin.                                                                                   |\\n| `<<WORKARENA_PLUS_VERSION>>`               | WorkArena++ release pin.                                                                                  |\\n| `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>`                    | Per-task step cap.                                                                                        |\\n| `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>`                | Observation truncation cap (default 8000).                                                                |\\n| `<<THOUGHT_TOKEN_BUDGET>>`                  | Maximum tokens spent on the `Thought:` block per step (default 256).                                      |\\n| `<<KAPPA_THRESHOLD>>`                      | Judge-vs-programmatic \\u03ba below which a task is downgraded (default 0.6).                                   |\\n| `<<N_PERMUTATION_TASKS>>`                  | Number of tasks on which the contamination permutation sub-check is run (default 5).                      |\\n| `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>` block   | Any benchmark number (task count, L1/L2/L3 fraction, baseline success rate, etc.) \\u2014 never asserted.       |\\n\\n**Everything above is design, not evidence.** No run was executed; no episode traces, success rates, step counts, or token costs are reported.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"All design choices are grounded in (i) the workspace inspection from turn 1 (`find /workspace -type f` returned only `task.json`; no WorkArena++, BrowserGym, or related package present; no GPU; network disabled in bubblewrap; `pip3 list` shows no LLM/agent SDKs); (ii) the researcher-approved bindings on arms (exactly two), models (single frontier, identifier left as `<<MODEL_ID>>`), and subset (rule (ii), 10 per level, enumeration left as `<<TASK_ENUMERATION>>`); (iii) the design's own paired-MacNemar-plus-sign-test defence for binary outcomes and the n=30 power computation stated explicitly. No benchmark counts, fractions, version pins, or baseline rates are cited from memory; each appears as a labelled placeholder `<<ASSUMPTION_PENDING_RESEARCHER>>`. The missing `neurips2024/97713/3:setup_1` is treated as not in scope and is not invented.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1. Expected empirical outcome is unknown; H1/H2/H3 are qualitative directions, not parameter-value predictions.\", \"A2. API budget, per-token prices, and per-episode wall-clock are unstated; \\u00a79.2 gives a parametric budget but no numeric total.\", \"A3. No GPU + no network in bubblewrap \\u21d2 open-model arm is structurally excluded; design is single-actor, cross-vendor deferred.\", \"A4. BrowserGym's default prompt does not already contain a `Thought:` field; the user-message injection point is therefore additive. If the researcher's reflection on `<<BROWSERGYM_VERSION>>` shows otherwise, \\u00a74.1 must be revised to a syntactic-prefix intervention.\", \"A5. Programmatic validators exist for the chosen 30 tasks; if any task lacks one, it falls to sensitivity per \\u00a77.3.\", \"A6. n_trials=3 paired within (task, seed) provides honest per-task success aggregation; we do not assume n_trials=3 eliminates all API non-determinism.\", \"A7. `<<OBS_TRUNCATION_TOKENS>>`=8000 is a parameter, not a fact: divergence in truncation rate across arms is a primary confound-mitigation concern (\\u00a77.1).\", \"A8. L1/L2/L3 fractions in the published benchmark are not asserted; the design's 1/3-per-level split is a methodological choice, not a claim about the published mix.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"U1. The exact `<<MODEL_ID>>` and `<<JUDGE_MODEL_ID>>` (they must differ).\", \"U2. The exact `<<TASK_ENUMERATION>>` over `(level \\u00d7 app)` cells.\", \"U3. `<<BROWSERGYM_VERSION>>`, `<<WORKARENA_PLUS_VERSION>>`, `<<MAX_STEPS_PER_TASK>>` \\u2014 every numeric reference to the framework is placeholder.\", \"U4. Token price, latency, and budget ceiling \\u2014 no numeric total can be promised.\", \"U5. Whether the chosen 30-task subset is fully covered by programmatic validators; if not, \\u00a77.3 governs.\", \"U6. Whether the researcher's submission surface expects an analysis script skeleton alongside the design \\u2014 none is requested here but can be added in a follow-up.\", \"U7. Whether `track.md` imposes hard caps on design-document length or section count beyond what has been provided.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-b5a51943-8214-462d-a7da-aedf4ebf7c55", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
